[PERSISTENT EXPERIENCE GUIDANCE]
## Consolidated Experience from Similar Tasks
Key consolidated lessons: (1) Before any pick-up, verify the target object exists, is visible or known to be reachable, and is near enough for the robot to grasp; if not, navigate to its location first. (2) Before any place action, verify the robot is actually holding the target object; a failed pick means a place will also fail. Confirm grasp success after picking, and if not holding, retry the pick rather than attempting to place. (3) Before issuing a move command, check that the intended path is clear of obstacles; if blocked, replan around, remove, or otherwise avoid the obstruction instead of assuming forward movement will succeed. (4) Validate the expected state after each action (object acquired, robot holding, location reached, object placed) so failures are detected early and corrected before continuing.

## Preliminary Thinking
For moving the spoon from the right counter to the right receptacle of the left counter: First, perceive the scene to locate the spoon on the right counter, the target right receptacle on the left counter, and any obstacles between the robot's current position and those locations. Navigate to the spoon on the right counter; before moving, verify the path is clear, and if blocked, replan around the obstacle. Once near the spoon, confirm it is present and within reach, then execute the pick-up action. Verify the robot is actually holding the spoon; if not, reassess the spoon's location/reachability and retry the pick. With the spoon held, navigate to the left counter and specifically to its right receptacle, again checking the path for obstacles and replanning if blocked. Confirm the receptacle location and that the spoon is still held, then execute the place action into the right receptacle. Finally, verify the spoon has been successfully placed in the intended receptacle. If any step fails, re-perceive the environment and adjust the plan accordingly.
[/PERSISTENT EXPERIENCE GUIDANCE]